    "evidence_text": "Introduction\tKnowledge Base Question Answering (KBQA) systems answer questions by obtaining information from KB tuples  ,  ,  ,  ,  ,  .\n\nFor an input question, these systems typically generate a KB query, which can be executed to retrieve the answers from a KB.\n\nFor example, the SimpleQuestions  data set has 14%\n\nof the golden test relations not observed in golden training tuples.\n\nThis paper improves KB relation detection to cope with the problems mentioned above.\n\nIn order to assess how the proposed improved relation detection could benefit the KBQA end task, we also propose a simple KBQA\n\nimplementation composed of two-step relation detection.\n\nFinally the highest scored query from the above steps is used to query the KB for answers.\n\nHowever, while the questions are natural\n\nWe have this step because we have observed that entity linking sometimes becomes a bottleneck in KBQA systems.\n\nHaving observed that different entity candidates usually connect to different\n\nThe KBQA end task, as a result, benefits from this process.\n\nboth tasks.\n\nWebQSP (WQ): A multi-relation KBQA task.\n\nWe use the entire Freebase KB for evaluation purposes.\n\nThe third block of the table details two ablation tests for the proposed components in our\n\nKBQA systems: (1) Removing the entity re-ranking step significantly decreases the scores.\n\nAppendix C gives the detailed performance of the re-ranking step.\n\nConclusion\tKB relation detection is a key step in KBQA and is significantly different from general relation extraction tasks.\n\nWe propose a novel KB relation detection model,\n\nHR-BiLSTM, that performs hierarchical matching between questions and KB relations.",
